course: space
lesson: 17
title: Stars: what they are
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Sun is a star, quite an ordinary one: it looks so huge and bright only because it is about 270,000 times closer to us than the next nearest star.
explain: A star is a giant ball of hot gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, that makes its own light: deep in the Sun's core, at about 15 million °C, hydrogen is squeezed into helium and gives out enormous energy.
explain: A star's colour tells its temperature, just like a piece of metal in a fire that glows dull red, then yellow, then white as it gets hotter: red stars are coolest and blue-white stars hottest.
explain: Stars twinkle because their light passes through moving layers of warm and cool air that bend it this way and that, like a coin at the bottom of a wobbling pool.
explain: Most stars in our galaxy are dim red dwarfs, too faint to see with the naked eye, and many stars come in pairs or groups, like Sirius, the brightest star of the night, and its tiny white dwarf partner.
fact: The Sun is a star, an ordinary one, and it looks so different from the others only because it is about 270,000 times closer than the next nearest star.
fact: A star is a huge ball of hot gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, that makes its own light.
fact: Deep in the Sun's core, at about 15 million °C, hydrogen nuclei are squeezed together to make helium, releasing enormous energy.
fact: The Sun is about 109 times wider than the Earth, and about 1.3 million Earths could fit inside it.
fact: Star colours show their temperature: red stars are coolest at about 3,000 °C, the Sun is yellow-white at about 5,500 °C, and blue-white stars can be over 20,000 °C.
fact: Stars twinkle because their light passes through moving layers of air of different temperatures that bend it this way and that.
fact: Most stars in our galaxy are red dwarfs, smaller, cooler and dimmer than the Sun, and not one of them is bright enough to see with the naked eye.
fact: Sirius in Canis Major is the brightest star in the night sky, about 25 times brighter than the Sun in real power and about 8.6 light years away (a light year is the distance light travels in a year), which is near for a star.
fact: Many stars are not alone but come in pairs or groups orbiting each other, and Sirius has a tiny white dwarf partner.
fact: On a very dark, clear night away from towns, you can see about 2,000 to 3,000 stars at once with the naked eye, but in a city only a few dozen, because street lights make the sky glow and hide the faint stars.
fact: Many bright stars have Arabic names, such as Aldebaran, Betelgeuse and Altair, because medieval astronomers in the Islamic world kept and improved Greek star catalogues.
exercise: Is the Sun a star? | Yes, an ordinary star that happens to be close to us | No, the Sun is a planet made of fire | No, stars are much smaller than the Sun | Yes, and it is the biggest star there is
exercise: Why does the Sun look so different from the other stars? | It is about 270,000 times closer than the next nearest star | It is made of different stuff from the stars | It is the only star that makes its own light | It is the biggest and hottest star of all
exercise: What is a star? | A huge ball of hot gas that makes its own light | A big round rock that reflects the light of the Sun | A hole in the sky letting light through | A small fire burning in space
exercise: What are stars mostly made of? | Hydrogen and helium | Rock and iron | Burning coal and oil | Oxygen and water
exercise: What happens deep in the Sun's core? | Hydrogen is squeezed together to make helium | Coal burns with a huge flame that never goes out | Lava erupts from volcanoes | Water boils into steam
exercise: About how hot is the Sun's core? | About 15 million °C | About 100 °C | About 5,500 °C | About 1,000 °C, like lava
exercise: About how many Earths could fit inside the Sun? | About 1.3 million | About 109, side by side | About 1,000 Earths | Only about 10 or so
exercise: About how many times wider than the Earth is the Sun? | About 109 times | About 9 times | About 1,000,000 times | About twice
exercise: What colour are the coolest stars? | Red | Blue-white | Yellow | Green
exercise: About how hot is the Sun's surface? | About 5,500 °C | About 15 million °C | About 500 °C | About 50,000 °C
exercise: Why do stars twinkle? | Moving layers of air bend their light this way and that | They switch on and off as they burn | They spin very fast like lighthouses | Birds and clouds keep passing in front of them
exercise: What kind of star is most common in our galaxy? | Red dwarfs | Stars like the Sun | Blue giants | White dwarfs
exercise: Which is the brightest star in the night sky? | Sirius | Polaris, the Pole Star | Betelgeuse | The Moon
exercise: How far away is Sirius? | About 8.6 light years | About 8.6 kilometres | About as far as the Moon | About a million light years
exercise: What is special about Sirius's partner? | It is a tiny white dwarf orbiting with it | It is a planet with life on it, like the Earth | It is a black hole that swallows light | It is the Sun
exercise: About how many stars can you see on a very dark, clear night away from towns? | About 2,000 to 3,000 | About 20 | Millions | Exactly 88, one for each constellation
exercise: Why do many bright stars have Arabic names like Aldebaran and Altair? | Astronomers in the medieval Islamic world kept and improved Greek star catalogues | Arabic astronomers made all the stars | The stars can only be seen from Arabia | The names were made up for a film in the 1900s
test: My little sister says the Sun isn't a star cos stars are tiny and come out at night. what do you tell her | The Sun is a star; the others just look tiny because they are so far away | She is right, the Sun is a planet | Stars only shine at night, so the Sun can't be one | The Sun is a star that is smaller than all the others
test: In a cartoon a star has five points and a smiley face. What does a real star look like up close? | A huge round ball of glowing gas | A five-pointed piece of shiny rock | A flat disc of ice | A tiny spark like a firework
test: A star glows blue-white and another glows red. Which is hotter? | The blue-white one | The red one | They are both the same | You cannot tell anything from colour
test: Why can you see far more stars in the countryside than in the city? | Town lights make the sky too bright to see faint stars | There really are more stars above the countryside than towns | City air has no stars in it | Stars avoid shining on cities
test: are you thick or what, Sirius is obviously the closest star cos it's brightest. right? | No, it is bright because it is powerful and fairly near; the Sun and others are closer | Yes, the brightest star is always the closest | Yes, Sirius is closer than the Sun | No, Sirius is not a star but a planet
test: If you looked at the stars from the Moon, which has no air, would they twinkle? | No, with no air to bend the light they would shine steadily | Yes, even more than from the Earth | Yes, exactly the same as from the Earth | No, you could not see any stars at all
test: Most stars in the galaxy are red dwarfs. Why can't we see any of them without a telescope? | They are all too dim | They are hidden behind the Sun | They only shine in the daytime | They are black and give no light
test: Which is true? | Many stars come in pairs or groups orbiting each other | Every star is alone in space | Stars never orbit anything | Only the Sun has a partner star, which is too dim for us to see
test: what makes the sun shine, is it like a massive bonfire | No, hydrogen is squeezed into helium in its core | Yes, it burns coal and wood | Yes, it burns oxygen like a candle | No, it reflects the light that comes from other stars
test: If the Sun were as far away as the next nearest star, what would it look like? | Just one more star in the night sky | Still a huge bright disc, only a bit smaller | A dark circle with no light | A planet with rings
test: Aldebaran, Betelgeuse and Altair all have names from which language? | Arabic | Latin | Chinese | Greek
exam: Identify the principal process by which energy is generated within the core of the Sun. | The fusion of hydrogen nuclei to form helium | The combustion of coal and other fuels in its core | The reflection of starlight | The eruption of volcanoes
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the colour of stars. | It indicates their temperature, red being coolest and blue-white hottest | It indicates their age, red being youngest | It indicates their distance, blue being nearest | It bears no relation to any physical property
exam: State the principal reason for the twinkling of stars as observed from the surface of the Earth. | The refraction of their light by moving layers of air | The intermittent extinction and rekindling of their light | The rapid rotation of the stars | The passage of birds across their light
exam: Identify the star which presents the greatest apparent brightness in the night sky. | Sirius | Polaris | Betelgeuse | Aldebaran
